@startuml LogiShop_MVC_Figure34
!theme plain
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam ArrowColor #2E7D32
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

title Figure 3.4. Illustration of MVC design pattern — LogiShop

skinparam participant {
  BackgroundColor #E8F5E9
  BorderColor #2E7D32
}

actor "User" as U

participant "Controller\n(API routes, auth,\norchestration in lib)" as C #C8E6C9

participant "Model\n(entities, validation,\nSupabase access, rules)" as M #C8E6C9

participant "View\n(Next.js pages &\ncomponents)" as V #C8E6C9

database "Database\n(Supabase / PostgreSQL)" as DB #F8BBD9

== Request / response cycle (conceptual) ==

U -> C : (1) Request\nSubmit form, open page,\nsync tracking, …

C -> M : (2) Contacts\nValidation, persistence,\ncarrier orchestration

M <-> DB : Read / write\n{ Abstraction layer }\n(Supabase client, serializers)

M --> C : (3) Returns\nOrders, status, DTOs

C -> V : (4) Delivers\nData for render\n(JSON / props)

V -> U : (5) Shows\nLists, map, forms,\nstatus messages

note bottom
  In a Next.js monolith, the View (browser React) usually reaches the Controller through HTTP\n  requests to `app/api/**`. Shared Model logic in `lib/` keeps validation, roles, and data access\n  consistent for orders, tracking sync, and staff workflows.
end note

@enduml
